What Is the Internet?
The internet is a global network of interconnected networks — millions of private, public, and enterprise networks linked together, all following a common set of rules (protocols) so any device can communicate with any other. 

The word itself is short for interconnected networks.

Internet ≠ World Wide Web. The internet is the infrastructure (cables, routers, protocols).  The Web is just one service that runs on top of it (along with email, DNS, streaming, etc.).

How It Works — In 5 Steps
1. Physical Layer: Bits on the Wire
All data — a photo, a video, a text message — is converted into bits (1s and 0s) and travels as:

Pulses of light through fiber-optic cables (underground + undersea)
Electrical signals through copper wires
Radio waves (Wi-Fi, cellular, satellite)
2. Data Is Split Into Packets
Instead of sending one big file, the internet splits data into small packets.  Each packet carries:

A source IP address (where it came from)
A destination IP address (where it's going)
A sequence number (so the receiver can reassemble them in order)
The actual data chunk
Packets can take different routes and arrive out of order — that's fine, the receiver reassembles them.

3. Routers Forward Packets
Routers are the "post offices" of the internet. Each one:

Reads the destination IP on the packet
Looks up its routing table to find the best next hop
Forwards the packet to that next router
This happens hundreds of times between your device and the destination. At the ISP level, the Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is how ISPs exchange information about which networks are reachable.

4. The Protocol Stack (TCP/IP)
The internet runs on a layered protocol suite — each layer does one job:

Layer	Key Protocols	Job
Application	HTTP, DNS, SSH, SMTP	What is being communicated (a web page, an email)
Transport	TCP / UDP	How reliably — TCP guarantees order & delivery; UDP is fast but fire-and-forget
Internet	IP (IPv4/IPv6)	Where — assigns addresses, routes packets
Network Access	Ethernet, Wi-Fi	How physically — sends bits over the wire

Key distinction:

IP is best-effort — no guarantees on delivery, order, or integrity
TCP adds reliability on top: sequence numbers, acknowledgments, retransmission 
5. The Full Journey (Example: Typing a URL)
When you type https://www.example.com:

① DNS Lookup
   Browser asks: "What's the IP of www.example.com?"
   → DNS servers answer: 93.184.216.34

② TCP Handshake (3-way)
   Client → Server:  SYN
   Server → Client:  SYN-ACK
   Client → Server:  ACK
   (If HTTPS → TLS handshake to encrypt)

③ HTTP Request Sent
   GET / HTTP/1.1
   Host: www.example.com

④ Packets Travel
   Your router → ISP → backbone routers → server's network → server
   (each router reads the IP, forwards to next hop)

⑤ Server Responds
   Returns the page as HTTP response packets

⑥ Reassembly & Display
   Your TCP stack reassembles packets in order
   Browser renders the page

Total time: typically 20–200 ms.

Key Concepts at a Glance
Concept	What It Does
IP Address	Unique identifier for every device (like a house address)
DNS	Translates human-readable names → IP addresses (like a phone book)
Router	Forwards packets between networks based on destination IP
BGP	The "routing protocol of the internet" — how ISPs share reachability info
TCP	Reliable, ordered delivery (web, email, file transfer)
UDP	Fast, unreliable delivery (video calls, DNS, gaming)
Packet	Small, self-contained chunk of data, independently routable
CDN	Caches content at edge locations close to users (we covered this earlier)

The One-Sentence Summary
The internet works by splitting data into small packets, giving each packet a destination address, routing them hop-by-hop through routers using the TCP/IP protocol suite, and reassembling them at the destination — all happening in milliseconds, billions of times per second.

This is the foundation everything else builds on: cloud regions, availability zones, CDNs, load balancers, Kubernetes networking — it's all just packets finding their way from A to B.
